So, I'm cruising happily along in the mines, having walked comfortably
through it as a gnomish archaeologist guarded by a pet minotaur and a
housecat to boot. On my way back up, a poly trap gets my minotaur, and
turns him into a kobold zombie, but turns the housecat into a Titan.
Sweet.

I had a close call with a mumak while the titan was eating that took
me down to 24 of 60 HP, and was then digging a passage for easier
travel between staircases, when I saw a glow off in the distant dark
of the mine passage. Interesting, I thought. Could be a yellow light,
or a sphere, I should watch for that. I saw the glow get closer but
didn't see anything, so I kept digging, figuring I had a good line of
sight and I would stop before anything got there, and hey, my Titan
was right next to me anyway, so I'm safe, right?

Wrong. My Titan saw it, engaged it, and you can guess what it was.
Invisible black light.

So... it exploded. My Titan got confused, and hit me and cast a spell,
either one of which was probably enough to do me in by that point.

Now, whenever any of my pets have been hit by an invisible black light
before, the game has always said "<pet> looks confused".  Confusion
and hallucination are two very different things for players, but I
wonder if they are for pets. If they aren't, the pet in question
shouldn't be able to cast a spell, right? But if they are, why not
give a message more like "<pet> looks freaked out."

Not that the information differentiation would have mattered much in
this case.

Hmmm. If I'd survived my Titan's initial onslaught, could I have
Elberethed the floor and kept him off my back until he was OK? Or
would he have self-healed himself out of hallucination?

